The row has an increasing part and a decreasing part. For row `i`, print `1` through `i`, then print `2 * (n - i)` spaces, and finish with `i` down to `1`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void pattern12(int n) {
        for (int row = 1; row <= n; row++) {
            for (int value = 1; value <= row; value++) {
                cout << value;
            }

            for (int space = 1; space <= 2 * (n - row); space++) {
                cout << ' ';
            }

            for (int value = row; value >= 1; value--) {
                cout << value;
            }

            cout << '\n';
        }
    }
};
```

For `n = 4`, the rows are `1      1`, `12    21`, `123  321`, and `12344321`.

The loops only print the required characters. If `n` is `0`, the method does not print anything.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because the total width of all rows is checked across the pattern.

Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the output.

This uses a `StringBuilder` for each line. The first loop adds the rising digits, the second adds the empty middle, and the last loop adds the falling digits.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern12(int n) {
        for (int row = 1; row <= n; row++) {
            StringBuilder line = new StringBuilder();

            for (int value = 1; value <= row; value++) {
                line.append(value);
            }

            for (int count = 0; count < 2 * (n - row); count++) {
                line.append(' ');
            }

            for (int value = row; value >= 1; value--) {
                line.append(value);
            }

            System.out.println(line);
        }
    }
}
```

The important part is that the gap becomes smaller by two spaces on every new row. For example, the last row has no middle gap and becomes `12344321` when `n` is `4`.

An input of `n = 1` still works and prints `11`.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the current `StringBuilder`.

The pattern can also be printed one row at a time through recursion. The recursive call moves to the next row, while small helper functions print the three parts of the current row.

```cpp
class Solution {
    void printIncreasing(int value, int limit) {
        if (value > limit) {
            return;
        }

        cout << value;
        printIncreasing(value + 1, limit);
    }

    void printDecreasing(int value) {
        if (value == 0) {
            return;
        }

        cout << value;
        printDecreasing(value - 1);
    }

    void printRows(int row, int n) {
        if (row > n) {
            return;
        }

        printIncreasing(1, row);

        for (int count = 0; count < 2 * (n - row); count++) {
            cout << ' ';
        }

        printDecreasing(row);
        cout << '\n';

        printRows(row + 1, n);
    }

public:
    void pattern12(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        printRows(1, n);
    }
};
```

The base case stops after row `n`. With `n = 2`, the first recursive row prints `1  1`, and the next row prints `1221`.

This is mainly a different way to organize the same printing work. Recursion makes the code less compact than loops, and it uses call stack space.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` because of the recursive calls.

